<sn><md>#### Primary Structure $1^\circ$:

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
* Represents the `linear sequence of amino acids` connected by ++peptide bonds++ within a *polypeptide chain*.
* It possesses an `N-terminus` (amino end) and a `C-terminus` (carboxyl end).
* The `precise sequence` dictates the **identity and function** of the protein; altering even a single amino acid can change the entire protein's function.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcS_9gvxB00rlUeVCrIYZnue-YR8wkP7Fq7DMz_2X2YoscC2jnWI7M8osBRb&amp;s=10)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### **Secondary Structure $2^\circ$:**

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-9
* Refers to local `folding or coiling` of the polypeptide backbone, stabilized by `hydrogen bonds` formed between ++nearby amino acids++.
* **Two major forms:**
  * $\alpha$-Helix:** A `right-handed clockwise` coil common in structural proteins like ++keratin++ (found in hair and nails).
  * $\beta$-Pleated Sheet:** Polypeptide chains `running side-by-side in parallel` or anti-parallel orientations, joined by `hydrogen bonds` (found in silk protein, **fibroin**).
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-3
![img](https://microbenotes.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/Secondary-Structure-of-Protein.jpeg)
:::
:::

</md></sn><sn><md>### Tertiary Structure $3^\circ$

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
* The overall **three-dimensional (3D) shape** of a single polypeptide chain formed by `folding and bending`.
* Determined by `interactions between the R` side chains.
* Governs the `biological activity` of the protein and determines whether it is `fibrous or globular`.
* **Bonds involved:** 
 Hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges, hydrophobic interactions, and Van der Waals forces.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://studymind.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/Screen-Shot-2022-03-29-at-12.52.48-PM.png)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Quaternary Structure $4^\circ$

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
* The `spatial arrangement` and assembly of `two or more polypeptide chains (subunits)` forming a single functional protein complex.
* Held together by the `same non-covalent and covalent` interactions found in tertiary structures.
* **Key Examples:**
  * **Hemoglobin:** Composed of **4 subunits** $2\alpha$ chains and $2\beta$ chains).
  * **Insulin:** Composed of **2 subunits** (A and B chains) linked by disulfide bridges.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://themedicalbiochemistrypage.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/hemoglobin.jpg)
:::
:::



</md></sn><sn><md>### Protien Shape

Based on these four structure we have 
- Fibrous
- Globular

Shaped Protien.

![img](https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2025/09/52088_httpscdn-savemyexams-comuploads202012globular-and-fibrous.png)

| Feature | Fibrous Proteins | Globular Proteins |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Structure** | Primary or Secondary | Tertiary,Quaternary |
| **Type** | Structural | Functional |
| **Solubility** | Water-insoluble | Mostly water-soluble |
| **Physical Nature** | Tough | Fragile in nature |
| **Primary Function** | Used to build a variety of materials that support and protect specific tissues. | Function as enzymes, hormones, and antibodies. |
| **Examples** | Skin, hair, fingernails, and keratin | Enzymes, hormones, and antibodies |</md></sn>